-- Capabilities widening, Task 2 -- Check 4 (transitive `use`), the ACCEPT
-- side. `Vault` (`stdlib/vault.march:26`) declares `needs IO.Mut`. A module
-- that `use`s `Vault` AND REFERENCES a function requiring `IO.Mut` inherits an
-- obligation to cover `IO.Mut` too -- `check_module_needs`'s Check 4 walks
-- every `DUse`, works out which capabilities the referenced functions require,
-- and requires each of THOSE caps to be covered -- via the same `cap_subsumes`
-- subsumption Check 1 uses -- by the IMPORTING module's own `needs`. Here
-- `Main` declares `needs IO.Mut` itself, so the transitive obligation from
-- `use Vault` + `Vault.new` is satisfied (`cap_subsumes "IO.Mut" "IO.Mut"`
-- holds reflexively). See reject/t39 for the same shape with the `needs
-- IO.Mut` line removed. See core-march-types.md 2.8.6.
--
-- The `Vault.new` reference is load-bearing as of 2026-08-06: Check 4
-- propagation is DEMAND-DRIVEN, so an importer inherits only the capabilities
-- of the functions it actually references. This fixture's body used to be a
-- bare `()` with no reference at all, which now (correctly) costs nothing --
-- meaning it would have accepted even if Check 4 were deleted outright. A
-- witness that passes with the mechanism removed proves nothing, so the
-- reference is what keeps this an ACCEPT witness for Check 4 rather than for
-- the mere presence of a `use`.
mod Main do
  needs IO.Mut
  use Vault

  fn main(_cap_mut : Cap(IO.Mut)) do
    let _ = Vault.new("t")
    ()
  end
end
